Three housekeeping items the consistency audit left open.

1. The dossier's preamble had become a wall of qualifications a reader met
   before any evidence. It now opens by saying plainly what the file IS -- the
   sealed August 2026 record, never edited, because a record whose contents
   change is not a record -- and what it is NOT: the project's current
   position on anything. Where the sealed text and a live file disagree, the
   live file governs. A twelve-row CORRECTIONS TABLE follows: each superseded
   claim, where it sits, what is now true, and the file that owns it -- the
   v3.3 filename, chapter 04's open pins, the startup answer, the headcount
   language, the Irregular tie (correct as sealed, and restated because a
   22 Aug draft briefly overwrote it), the incident count at five, the
   victim-disclosed-first order, the two twelves, the unpinned Mexican-agencies
   incident, the 25 Aug watch still owed, and the two sourceless valuations.
   The long-form notes follow the table for anyone wanting the reasoning.

2. The census tally counted six read rows while USING California SB 53's
   findings in the rows below -- the officers-and-directors line cited SB 53
   inside a denominator SB 53 was not in. SB 53 was read in full on 21 August,
   by a human eye, from the chaptered text, graded ✅. It is the seventh row;
   'actually frontier bills' becomes 5 of 7. This is the Illinois artefact
   repeated, so the file now carries the standing rule that prevents a third:
   a row graded ✅ enters the count in the same edit that grades it. The
   tally's promise is that it never exceeds the rows read; the corollary --
   that it must not fall short of them -- is the half that failed twice.

3. changelog: the two 22 August entries swapped into newest-first order (the
   enterprise pass was the day's second turn); the 21 August restructure entry
   moved out of the sealed 19 August block, where it had been written after
   the merge and sat out of order inside content declared verbatim; and the
   sealed block gains the closing marker it never had, so the boundary of the
   verbatim content is visible.

Tagged v3.4 text unchanged (E10).
